+## Why the hook URL is checked before it's called
+
+`deployHookUrl` is plain text a customer fills in, not a URL this app chose.
+Before firing a request at it, `triggerDeployHook` requires a plain
+`https://` URL whose host resolves to a public address, and calls `fetch`
+with `redirect: "manual"` so a hook can't bounce the request somewhere else.
+Without that check, a hook pointed at `localhost`, an RFC1918 address, or a
+cloud metadata endpoint (`169.254.169.254`) would turn a publish into a
+request against our own infrastructure, made with our server's network
+access — the customer supplies the URL, but the request is ours. A URL that
+fails the check is treated the same as an exhausted retry: `deployTriggered`
+comes back `false`, logged once at `error` rather than retried, since retrying
+a config problem three times gains nothing a real network blip would.
+
